## File 5: `middleware/errorHandler.js`

**Why a dedicated error-handling middleware?** Express lets you define a special 4-argument function `(err, req, res, next) => {}` that acts as a catch-all. By centralizing error formatting here, every route handler stays focused on business logic instead of repeating JSON error shapes.

```javascript
/**
 * middleware/errorHandler.js
 *
 * Global Express error-handling middleware.
 *
 * How it works:
 *   1. Any route can call `next(err)` or throw an Error (in async routes, wrap with express-async-handler).
 *   2. This middleware inspects the error and returns a consistent JSON payload.
 *   3. Unknown errors get a generic message so stack traces never leak to clients.
 */

/**
 * @param {Error} err   The error object (may be our custom APIError or a native Error).
 * @param {Object} req  Express request (unused here, but required by Express signature).
 * @param {Object} res  Express response object.
 * @param {Function} next  Express next function (not used in error handler).
 */
function errorHandler(err, req, res, next) {
  // Determine the HTTP status code: custom errors carry statusCode; default to 500.
  const statusCode = err.statusCode || 500;

  // Build a clean JSON response body.
  const responseBody = {
    error: {
      message: err.message,
      // Include the error name for easier client-side debugging (optional).
      type: err.name || 'InternalServerError',
    },
  };

  // In development, also include the stack trace so developers can debug locally.
  if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production' && err.stack) {
    responseBody.error.stack = err.stack;
  }

  res.status(statusCode).json(responseBody);
}

module.exports = errorHandler;
```

