## Summary of Patterns & Why They Were Chosen

| Pattern | Where Used | Why |
|---------|-----------|-----|
| **Repository Pattern** | `models/books.js` | Decouples routes from storage; swap array for DB later with zero route changes |
| **Service Layer** | Route handlers call model functions | Keeps handlers focused on HTTP concerns (status codes, validation), not data logic |
| **Error-Handling Middleware** | `middleware/errorHandler.js` | Single catch-all for all errors; no try/catch boilerplate in every route |
| **Async Handler Wrapper** | `asyncHandler(fn)` | Catches promise rejections automatically — Express doesn't do this natively |
| **Custom Error Classes** | `errors/customErrors.js` | Each error carries its own HTTP status, code, and metadata for consistent responses |
| **REST Conventions** | Route definitions | Standardized methods (GET/POST/PUT/DELETE) make the API predictable and documentable |
| **JSDoc Documentation** | Every function | Self-documenting code; works with IDE autocomplete and tools like Swagger |
| **In-Memory Array** | `models/books.js` | Zero setup for education; demonstrates patterns without infrastructure complexity |

