## 2. `server.js` — Application Entry Point & Middleware Setup

```javascript
/**
 * server.js
 *
 * This file bootstraps the Express application, configures middleware,
 * mounts route handlers, and starts the HTTP server.
 *
 * Why separate this from routes?
 *   - Keeps configuration (middleware, error handling) distinct from business logic.
 *   - Makes it easy to add global middleware (CORS, JSON parsing, logging) in one place.
 *   - Follows the "separation of concerns" principle: setup ≠ routing ≠ data access.
 */

const express = require('express');
const cors = require('cors');
const bookRoutes = require('./routes/books');

// Create an Express application instance.
// Each call to express() creates a new app with its own middleware stack.
const app = express();

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// MIDDLEWARE CONFIGURATION
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * CORS middleware: allows cross-origin requests from any origin.
 * In production you'd restrict this to specific domains for security.
 */
app.use(cors());

/**
 * JSON body parser: automatically parses incoming request bodies with
 * Content-Type: application/json into req.body.
 * Without this, POST/PUT bodies would be raw strings.
 */
app.use(express.json());

/**
 * Simple request logger middleware (educational).
 * Logs method, URL, and timestamp for every incoming request.
 * In production you'd use a dedicated logging library like winston or pino.
 */
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log(`[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${req.method} ${req.url}`);
  next(); // Pass control to the next middleware/route handler
});

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ROUTE MOUNTING
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * Mount book routes under /books.
 * All endpoints defined in routes/books.js will be prefixed with /books.
 * This keeps the main app file clean and allows route files to be tested independently.
 */
app.use('/books', bookRoutes);

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ERROR HANDLING MIDDLEWARE
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * Global error-handling middleware (4-parameter signature).
 * Express automatically routes unhandled errors here.
 *
 * Why 4 parameters?
 *   - The last parameter (err) distinguishes this from regular middleware.
 *   - It must be defined AFTER all routes so it catches errors from anywhere.
 *   - We don't call next() here—this is the final catch-all.
 */
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  // Default to 500 if no statusCode was set by a custom error class
  const statusCode = err.statusCode || 500;

  // In production you'd hide stack traces and internal details from clients
  const isDevelopment = process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production';

  res.status(statusCode).json({
    error: {
      message: err.message,
      ...(isDevelopment && { stack: err.stack }), // Only show stack in dev
    },
  });
});

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SERVER STARTUP
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`📚 Book API running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
  console.log(`   Try: GET  http://localhost:${PORT}/books`);
  console.log(`        POST http://localhost:${PORT}/books`);
  console.log(`        GET  http://localhost:${PORT}/stats`);
});
```

**Key design decisions explained:**  
- **Middleware order matters.** `cors()` and `express.json()` must run *before* routes so that request bodies are parsed before handlers read them.  
- **Error middleware has 4 parameters.** This is Express’s convention for distinguishing error-handling middleware from regular middleware (which has ≤3 params).  
- **Environment-aware error responses.** Stack traces leak implementation details; we only show them in development (`NODE_ENV !== 'production'`).  

